WEBVTT
NOTE Cedwyn film, frame 003, en. Timings are estimates from the script until the clip is rendered.

1
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:18.231
<v the villa steward>Look at it. Stone walls, a tiled roof, where my grandfather's grandfather talked of a round house of wattle and thatch. Same hill, same river below, and now a roof that won't lift off in a gale.

2
00:00:18.581 --> 00:00:27.427
<v a villa woman>Same mud, though. The lower rooms run wet every winter. And you walk these walls every morning as if they might wander off.

3
00:00:27.777 --> 00:00:36.623
<v the villa steward>Somebody has to. The masters count every tile when they ride out, and it's my back they look at when one is missing.

4
00:00:36.973 --> 00:00:50.435
<v a villa woman>Then come and count this. By the yard wall, where the men cut the new footings. A ring in the earth, dark soil, old post-holes. The old ones swear the round house stood just here.

5
00:00:50.785 --> 00:00:57.323
<v the villa steward>So we laid our floors on top of theirs. Makes you wonder who lays theirs on ours.

6
00:00:57.673 --> 00:00:59.596
<v a villa woman>Nobody. It's stone. Stone lasts.

7
00:00:59.946 --> 00:01:07.254
<v the villa steward>Stone lasts. Families don't always. Somebody's grandfather saw these walls go up, and somebody's grandchild will see them fall.

8
00:01:07.604 --> 00:01:12.604
<v a villa woman>Then let the hill keep its own count. Come in, the fire's lit.

9
00:01:12.954 --> 00:01:20.646
<v Nia>And here's that same hill on paper. Glamorgan Archives, D1230/7: excavations of the Roman settlement at Llandough-juxta-Penarth, recorded around 1968.

10
00:01:20.996 --> 00:01:34.073
<v Nia>Then the big dig. A salvage excavation in 1978 and '79, catalogued as 'Llandough Farm', HER event GGATE001109. The Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust dug south of the church, extensively, before housing association flats went up.

11
00:01:34.423 --> 00:01:44.423
<v Nia>Published in 1988, in BAR 188: a Roman villa, occupied from the second to the fourth century, and beneath it a possible late Iron Age roundhouse.

12
00:01:44.773 --> 00:01:53.619
<v Nia>This press file from 1980, GGAT00768s, the Llandough Roman Villa excavation, is catalogued as a separate site. Keep it in its own folder.

13
00:01:53.969 --> 00:02:08.585
<v Nia>What these show: people living in stone on this hill for two centuries and more. What they don't show: a single name, who held the land, or any thread to the farmhouse deeds. And 'possible' roundhouse means possible.

14
00:02:08.935 --> 00:02:15.088
<v Nia>Each dig keeps its own date. We'll open these again in 1968, 1978, 1980 and 1988.
